# cassette settings
# =================
#
# Every cassette program reads this file: the daemon, `cassette play` and
# `cassette list`, cassette-trim and cassette-tray. That is how they all
# agree, for example on where clips are.
#
# SYNTAX
#   - One `key = value` per line; spaces around `=` are optional.
#   - Values are used literally: no quotes, no $VARIABLES, and no comment
#     after a value (a `#` there becomes part of the value). Comments go on
#     their own line.
#   - Only a leading `~/` in `clips` is expanded, to the home folder.
#   - A key that is left out or commented out uses its default.
#   - Any mistake (unknown or misspelled key, empty value, bad number,
#     relative path) makes every cassette program refuse to run and name
#     the line. `cassette status` reports the same errors.
#
# APPLYING CHANGES
#   Nothing reloads by itself. Changes take effect when both services restart:
#
#     systemctl --user restart cassette cassette-tray
#
#   Restarting the daemon empties the buffer: the next clip only holds audio
#   recorded since the restart. Pausing and resuming from the tray does the
#   same.
#
# FINDING NODE NAMES (for mic and output)
#   The node name is the second column of:
#
#     pactl list short sources   # microphones (skip the *.monitor lines)
#     pactl list short sinks     # outputs
#
#   In qpwgraph or `wpctl status`, cassette's own recorders show up as
#   cassette-mic and cassette-output.
#
# THIS MACHINE (mutsu)
#   Audio chain: QuadCast -> EasyEffects -> PWSP Virtual Mic -> apps. Apps
#   play into EasyEffects' sink, which outputs to the HyperX headset. pwsp
#   plays soundboard sounds straight to the headset.
#
#   Current setup: only Discord is recorded (other people plus Discord's own
#   sounds). The mic and the soundboard are not (see `mic` below). This
#   depends on:
#     - Discord's Output Device (User Settings > Voice & Video) being
#       "Discord". Set to Default, Discord bypasses discord_sink
#       and clips are silent even though `cassette status` looks normal.
#     - The "Discord" sink comes from
#       ~/.config/pipewire/pipewire.conf.d/discord-sink.conf (in chezmoi).
#       It exists once PipeWire has restarted, such as at the next login.
#
#   The tray (left click) pauses by stopping the daemon. It stays paused
#   until resumed, or until the next login, when the service starts again.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# mic: the microphone to record
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   default   the system's default input, following any change to it
#   off       no microphone is recorded
#   <node>    exactly this PipeWire source, e.g. easyeffects_source
#
#   Default: default
#
#   Gotchas:
#   - While a mic is recorded it stays open, so "mic in use" indicators stay
#     on. Nothing is written to disk until a clip is saved.
#   - A <node> is recorded exactly. If it doesn't exist (a typo, the app that
#     provides it isn't running yet, the device is unplugged) or it
#     disappears, the daemon stops and systemd retries every 3 seconds until
#     the node is back. It never silently switches to another mic.
#     `journalctl --user -u cassette` then shows "defined target not found".
#   - A virtual mic that mixes in other sounds (a soundboard, for example)
#     records those too. If a recorded output plays the same sounds, they
#     end up in the clip twice, with a slight echo.
#   - mic and output can't both be off.
#
#   On this machine:
#     easyeffects_source   voice after EasyEffects (gate, noise
#                          removal, compressor): voice only
#     pwsp-virtual-mic     exactly what Discord receives: voice plus
#                          soundboard sounds
#     alsa_input.usb-Kingston_HyperX_Quadcast_4110-00.analog-stereo
#                          the raw QuadCast, before EasyEffects
#
mic = off


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# output: an output (sink) whose sound to record
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   default   whatever plays through the default output, which is all
#             desktop audio; follows the default output if it changes
#   off       no output is recorded
#   <node>    exactly this PipeWire sink. A sink that only one app plays
#             into (a dedicated "Discord" sink, say) records just that app.
#
#   Default: default
#
#   Gotchas:
#   - A <node> is recorded exactly, with the same stop-and-retry behavior as
#     mic.
#   - Everything played through the output is recorded, including
#     `cassette play` and cassette-trim playback when they play through it.
#   - If another program reroutes streams on a sink (EasyEffects does this on
#     the device it outputs to), naming that sink can make the recorder exit
#     at random with "defined target not found". `default` reaches that sink
#     reliably.
#
#   On this machine:
#     discord_sink   only Discord. pwsp plays soundboard sounds to the
#                    headset, not through Discord, so they are never in it.
#     default        everything on the HyperX headset. Naming the HyperX sink
#                    itself fails: EasyEffects reroutes streams on it and the
#                    recorder exits at random. `default` reaches it reliably.
#   output = default with mic = pwsp-virtual-mic records soundboard sounds
#   twice, once from each.
#
output = discord_sink


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# seconds: how much audio to keep
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   Any positive number; decimals are fine (12.5).
#
#   Default: 30
#
#   - A clip holds up to this much, less if the daemon started recently.
#   - Memory: about 11 MB per minute for each recorded source.
#   - Disk: about 11 MB per minute of clip. Clips are always 48 kHz 16-bit
#     stereo WAV; the format can't be changed.
#
seconds = 30


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# clips: where clips are saved
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   An absolute path, or one starting with ~/ . The folder is created, mode
#   700, when the first clip is saved; clips are mode 600.
#
#   Default: ~/.local/share/cassette/clips (or $XDG_DATA_HOME/cassette/clips)
#
#   - Changing it doesn't move clips that are already saved.
#   - Clips are never deleted automatically.
#   - Only files named clip-YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS.mmm.wav count as clips, or with
#     a name after the time, as cassette-trim names them:
#     clip-YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS.mmm-airhorn.wav. Other files are ignored by play,
#     list, cassette-trim and the tray.
#   - Saving in cassette-trim overwrites the clip in place; the untrimmed
#     audio is gone.
#
clips = ~/Audio/cassette


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# preview: how much audio cassette-trim plays after the right edge moves
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   A number of seconds, 0 or more; decimals are fine. 0 turns the preview
#   off.
#
#   Default: 0.5
#
#   - Moving the right edge plays this much audio leading up to it, which
#     shows exactly where the cut lands. Moving the left edge always plays
#     the whole selection; this setting doesn't affect it.
#   - Nothing plays while an edge is moving. Playback starts once the edge
#     has stayed put for half a second, so several quick adjustments only
#     play once. Moving an edge again stops whatever is playing.
#   - Only cassette-trim reads this; a running trim window keeps the value
#     it started with.
#
preview = 0.5
